<Site
  title="About · Ouijit"
  description="Ouijit is built by one person, Prentice Bjerkeseth. Free, open source under AGPL, no account and no telemetry, and the plan is to keep it that way.">
  <main class="compare-content about-content">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Running several coding agents at once changes what is hard. The code arrives faster than you can read
      it, and the work becomes keeping track: which agent is where, what changed, and what deserves your
      attention. Ouijit is for the senior engineer in that position who also cares how their tools look and
      feel. It keeps the git plumbing of branches and worktrees out of your way, stacks terminals instead of
      tiling them into a wall, gives each project its own avatar so you always know where you are, and makes
      its interactions tactile on purpose. If what you want is a tmux-style grid or a traditional editor, it
      isn't for you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ouijit is free and open source under AGPL-3.0, with no account, no telemetry, and a sandbox that runs on
      your own machine. If it ever charges for
      something, it will be a feature with a running cost of its own, such as hosted environments or sync. The
      licence keeps the code open and makes it hard for a company to take the work and sell it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I build Ouijit alone, and I build it with Ouijit. The <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> shows how often
      it ships. Many tools like this are raising venture money, getting acquired, or shutting down, and I intend
      to keep this one independent. Bugs and requests go to
      <a href="https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="about-sign"><a href="https://github.com/pbjer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prentice Bjerkeseth</a></p>
  </main>
</Site>
